(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"stars-between-the-strings":{"su-08":{"number":8,"title":"Jam Session","content":"The invitation to the jam session arrived on a paper napkin.\n\nGreg left it beneath my apartment door with a doodle of a guitar, a star, and what might have been a pancake wearing sunglasses. The message read: FRIDAY. COMMUNITY CENTER. BRING YOURSELF. OPTIONAL SNACKS. PLEASE DO NOT BRING A JUDGMENTAL SEAGULL.\n\nI brought myself and a bag of cinnamon rolls.\n\nThe community center was a low building near the beach with bright murals on the exterior. Inside, folding chairs had been pushed against the walls. Instruments filled the room: guitars, hand drums, a keyboard missing one key, and a collection of shakers that looked as though they had been assembled from kitchen supplies.\n\nGreg stood in the middle of it all, arguing with a woman in a yellow jacket about the correct way to arrange the chairs.\n\n“The circle is symbolic,” he said.\n\n“The circle is blocking the outlet,” she replied.\n\n“The outlet is part of the circle.”\n\nShe saw me and pointed. “Tell him.”\n\nI looked at Greg. “The outlet is part of the circle.”\n\nHe beamed. “See?”\n\n“That was not what I said.”\n\n“Your support means everything.”\n\nThe woman shook her head and moved the chairs. Greg crossed the room to me, smiling in a way that made it difficult to remember I had planned to act normal.\n\n“You came.”\n\n“You left a napkin under my door.”\n\n“A very persuasive napkin.”\n\nI held out the cinnamon rolls. “Peace offering for the outlet.”\n\n“I knew I loved you.”\n\n“You loved me before the pastries.”\n\n“Now I love you with evidence.”\n\nThe other musicians greeted me. Some knew Greg well. Some knew him only as the man who arrived early, brought snacks, and occasionally tried to turn equipment failures into songs. I was given a place in the circle and a shaker made from a plastic container filled with beans.\n\n“I don’t play,” I whispered to Greg.\n\n“Neither do half the people here.”\n\n“That is not reassuring.”\n\n“Music is not a test. It’s a conversation.”\n\n“I am bad at conversations.”\n\n“You’re excellent at them. You just think too hard before speaking.”\n\nThe session began with a rhythm simple enough to follow. Greg played guitar. The woman in the yellow jacket played hand drum. Someone else hummed a melody that wandered through the room and came back wearing different colors.\n\nI shook the container on the beat.\n\nAt first, I counted. One, two, three, four. My shoulders stayed tight. My eyes kept checking the other people, measuring whether I was too loud, too quiet, too slow.\n\nThen Greg looked at me.\n\nHe did not nod in correction. He smiled.\n\nThe rhythm was not a set of rules I could fail. It was something happening around me, leaving room for me to join.\n\nI stopped counting.\n\nThe song changed.\n\nAfterward, I realized I had been singing under my breath. Greg had heard me. Of course he had.\n\n“You have a beautiful voice,” he said.\n\n“You’re biased.”\n\n“I’m also a professional.”\n\n“You’re a professional musician, not a professional judge of my throat.”\n\n“I can be both if I take a certification course.”\n\n“Please don’t.”\n\nHe laughed and touched my wrist. “Would you sing with me?”\n\nThe room was still noisy around me, but the question made it narrow.\n\n“In front of everyone?”\n\n“Only if you want.”\n\n“What would I sing?”\n\n“Whatever you like.”\n\n“What if I choose something terrible?”\n\n“Then I’ll make it sound intentional.”\n\nI looked at the guitars, the chairs, the people who were busy talking and tuning. Nobody was watching me except Greg.\n\n“Maybe one song,” I said.\n\n“I’m learning that word has range.”\n\nHe led me to the small keyboard. One of the keys was missing, but the rest worked. Greg stood beside me, close enough that my arms brushed when I shifted.\n\n“You can choose the key,” he said.\n\n“I don’t know keys.”\n\n“You know the song.”\n\nHe played a few chords. I recognized the shape. It was the song I had told him about at the diner, the one that had found me on a bad day.\n\n“You remembered.”\n\n“I remember a lot about you.”\n\nThe room began to quiet as the others noticed. Panic flashed through me.\n\nGreg saw it. “We can stop.”\n\nI looked at the keyboard. Then at him.\n\n“No,” I said. “Ask me again.”\n\nHis eyes softened. “Do you want to sing this with me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe began the first line. His voice was warm and steady. I joined on the next phrase, quietly at first. The song carried my voice into the room and returned it changed, larger without becoming less mine.\n\nI did not look at the audience. I looked at Greg.\n\nHe watched me as though my voice were a secret he had been trusted to hold. When I stumbled over a note, he moved with me rather than correcting me. When I found the melody again, his smile widened.\n\nBy the final verse, I was singing.\n\nThe room applauded. Someone whistled. The woman in the yellow jacket called it beautiful and told Greg not to ruin it with a bridge.\n\nHe placed one hand over his heart. “I would never.”\n\n“You absolutely would,” I said.\n\n“Not in front of my beloved.”\n\nMy cheeks warmed.\n\nAfter the session, people shared food around a long table. Greg sat beside me, his knee touching mine beneath it. He did not move away. Every so often, his thumb brushed my knuckles where my hands rested together.\n\n“You were good,” he said.\n\n“You already told me.”\n\n“I have more evidence now.”\n\n“You are impossible.”\n\n“You love me.”\n\n“Unfortunately.”\n\n“Fortunately.”\n\nThe word was so certain that I looked at him.\n\nGreg's expression changed. “Sorry. That sounded like I was telling you how to feel.”\n\n“No.” I turned my hand beneath his, threading my fingers together. “It sounded like you were grateful.”\n\n“I am.”\n\nHe lifted my joined hands and kissed the back of mine. The gesture was visible to everyone, but there was no performance in it. He was not showing me off. He was simply loving me in the open.\n\nMy chest tightened with a happiness that frightened me.\n\n“What?” he asked.\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“That’s your face.”\n\n“What face?”\n\n“The one where something is beautiful and you’re waiting for it to be taken away.”\n\nI looked down at the table.\n\nGreg waited.\n\n“I don’t know how to have something good without preparing for the end,” I said.\n\n“You can prepare for the end and still enjoy the middle.”\n\n“That sounds exhausting.”\n\n“It is.” He squeezed my hand. “But we can take turns.”\n\nI looked at him again.\n\n“What does that mean?”\n\n“When you’re waiting for the end, I’ll remind you that we’re in the middle. When I’m scared that I’ll disappoint you, you can remind me that I’m still here.”\n\n“You get scared?”\n\n“Constantly. I’m just tall enough to hide it.”\n\nI laughed. He leaned close and kissed my temple.\n\n“There’s one more thing,” he said.\n\n“Is it about music?”\n\n“Kind of.”\n\nHe reached into his pocket and pulled out a small metal pick. It was worn smooth, engraved with a tiny star.\n\n“My son gave me this years ago,” Greg said. “I keep losing it and finding it again. It’s not valuable, but it helps me remember that songs don’t belong to the person who starts them. They keep changing hands.”\n\nHe placed it in my palm.\n\n“I can’t take that,” I said.\n\n“You’re not taking it. I’m giving it to you.”\n\n“What if I lose it?”\n\n“Then we find it again.”\n\nMy eyes stung.\n\nGreg leaned in. “Do you want to go somewhere quiet?”\n\nI nodded.\n\nOutside, the night smelled like salt and warm pavement. The community center lights glowed behind me. Greg slipped an arm around my shoulders. I leaned into him as I walked toward the water.\n\nAt the beach, he sat in the sand and pulled me between his knees. The metal pick rested safely in my pocket.\n\n“You sang,” he murmured.\n\n“I did.”\n\n“You looked terrified.”\n\n“I was.”\n\n“You did it anyway.”\n\nI turned my head. “You’re going to make this into a lesson.”\n\n“I’m going to make it into a kiss.”\n\n“That sounds more useful.”\n\nGreg kissed me beneath the stars. His hands moved with familiar care. When I shifted onto his lap, he asked. When I said yes, he held me closer.\n\nThe tide whispered against the shore. We kept kissing until the cold drove us back toward the road. We lingered on the sand while Greg searched for his missing shoe. He found it under the guitar case and shook out a small beach's worth of sand.\n\n“Great,” he said. “Former rock star, defeated by footwear.”\n\nI laughed, then stopped. “Before this becomes a song with a surprise ending, I need to tell you something.”\n\nHe hooked the guitar strap over one shoulder. “Okay. I'm listening.”\n\n“I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I can love more than one person, and I won't ask someone to date me under the impression that I'll eventually become exclusive.”\n\nGreg tucked his hair behind an ear. It promptly fell out again.\n\n“Wow. That's clear.” He hesitated. “I want to be clear too. I've done the thing where I act cool with everything because I'm afraid somebody will leave. Not my greatest hit.”\n\n“You don't have to agree tonight.”\n\n“Good. 'Cause I like you, but I've got questions. And some of 'em might be dumb.”\n\n“Ask them anyway.”\n\n“Do we have to make a calendar?”\n\n“Possibly.”\n\n“Oh, man. My natural enemy.”\n\nHe grinned, then took a breath. “Seriously, though. I'd rather figure out what I mean than say yes because you're cute and regret not being honest later.”\n\nI held out my hand. He took it. There was no instant agreement, no miraculous cure for jealousy, just a musician who could admit he was scared without pretending to be a philosopher.\n\nWe walked back to town with several questions still unanswered. I liked that he trusted me enough to leave them open.\n\n\nGreg walked me to the motel, kissed me goodnight at the door, and made me promise to text when I got inside.\n\nThe next morning, Greg sent me a voice message before he sent a text. His voice was sleep-rough and too close to the microphone.\n\n“Good morning,” he said. “I’m leaving this because I wanted you to hear me say that you sounded beautiful last night. Also because I burned toast and need someone to blame.”\n\nI listened twice before answering.\n\nYOU BURNT THE TOAST YOURSELF.\n\nHis reply arrived almost immediately.\n\nI WAS EMOTIONALLY DISTRACTED BY YOUR TALENT.\n\nI smiled at my phone. The warmth lasted through breakfast, through the walk to the community center, and into the afternoon when I found the plastic shaker in my bag.\n\nI had taken it without thinking. The container was cheap. The beans inside made a dry, uneven sound when I moved it. Still, it felt like proof that I had joined something.\n\nThe shaker had spent the week on my windowsill.\n\nAt first I'd thought of it as a souvenir. Then I remembered that it hadn't been offered to me. The community center used whatever it could afford; a handful of beans in a plastic container might have been somebody's careful contribution, not a free gift.\n\nI put it in my bag and wrote *ask if I can borrow this* on the same scrap of paper as my shopping list.\n\nGreg found the note while looking for a guitar pick. “Whoa. You making a whole contract with a jar of beans?”\n\n“It's a musical instrument.”\n\n“Sure is. One day it's gonna have an agent and refuse to go on stage without a rider.”\n\n“Will you represent it?”\n\n“Absolutely not. It already makes better financial decisions than I do.”\n\nHe laughed at his own joke, then watched me put the note back into the bag.\n\n“Seriously,” he said. “That was thoughtful. Pilar keeps those things around for people who don't own an instrument. It's easier to play when you don't have to buy your way in.”\n\n“I didn't mean to take it.”\n\n“I know. You can ask.”\n\n“Will she be upset?”\n\n“Maybe a little. She has a scary clipboard.”\n\nThe next session I told Pilar what had happened. She checked the lending sheet, drew a new row and let me sign it out for the week. I offered to buy her another container. She said the center would rather have someone who returned what they'd borrowed.\n\nGreg was already setting up chairs, carefully leaving the outlet clear. When he saw me come back with the loan recorded, he raised both hands as though I'd won a music award.\n\n“Ladies and gentlemen,” he announced, “the world's most responsible percussion soloist!”\n\n“Play your guitar,” I told him.\n\nHe did. His grin stayed with him through the first chord.\n\n\nAt the next session, I placed it on the table instead of hiding it in my pocket.\n\nThe woman in the yellow jacket noticed. “You keeping that?”\n\n“Apparently.”\n\n“Good. It has personality.”\n\nGreg arrived late with a box of pastries and a guilty expression. He looked at the shaker, then at me.\n\n“You brought your instrument.”\n\n“It has a personality problem.”\n\n“That makes two of us.”\n\nHe kissed my forehead in front of the circle. The gesture was quick, but it made me feel seen without making me feel displayed.\n\nWhen the music started, I found the beat faster this time. I still missed it occasionally. Greg still smiled. The song kept moving.\n\nAfterward, he tucked the shaker into my bag with exaggerated care.\n\n“You know,” he said, “we can find you a better one.”\n\n“This one has history.”\n\n“It’s been used once.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe looked at me with that warm, attentive expression. “I like seeing you collect evidence that you belong here.”\n\nI touched the metal pick in my pocket. “I like that you notice.”\n\n“I told you.”\n\n“You remember a lot.”\n\n“I do.”\n\nI kissed him under the community center awning while the last of the musicians carried instruments past me. The kiss was not a performance. It was simply another note in the conversation.","word_count":2363},"su-18":{"number":18,"title":"No More Running","content":"Greg ran when he was afraid.\n\nI learned this on a Tuesday afternoon, when he stopped answering messages after a disagreement about the future.\n\nThe disagreement had begun with a question about the van. I had asked whether he would ever consider selling it and moving into a real house. He had turned the question into a joke. I had asked again. He had gone quiet.\n\n“I’m not asking you to give up your life,” I said.\n\n“It’s not a life. It’s a van.”\n\n“You know what I mean.”\n\n“Do I?”\n\nThe tone sharpened.\n\n“I want to know whether you can imagine a future that isn’t built around leaving,” I said.\n\nGreg had looked away.\n\nThat was when I knew the question had reached somewhere real.\n\n“I need time,” he said.\n\n“Time to think or time to disappear?”\n\nHe flinched.\n\n“That was unfair,” I admitted.\n\n“It was accurate.”\n\nThen he left.\n\nAt first, I assumed he was taking a walk. Then an hour passed. Then two. His phone went to voicemail. The van was gone from the curb.\n\nI sat in the studio with the lights off and tried not to build a story from the silence.\n\nHe had left me. He had gotten tired. He had decided that love was easier when it stayed inside songs.\n\nBy evening, the story had become unbearable.\n\nI went to the car wash. The doors were locked. The community center was dark. Mara was not home. I walked to the overlook and stood beneath the trees, staring at the road.\n\nThe van appeared after midnight.\n\nGreg parked without turning off the engine. He climbed out and closed the door. His hair was windblown, his face exhausted.\n\n“You’re angry,” he said.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“You have every right.”\n\n“That doesn’t fix it.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI crossed my arms. “Where were you?”\n\n“Driving.”\n\n“For ten hours?”\n\n“I stopped twice.”\n\n“Did you tell me?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did you think about telling me?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“And?”\n\n“I didn’t.”\n\nThe honesty did not make the answer easier.\n\n“You don’t get to vanish because a question scares you,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You don’t get to make me sit in an empty room and guess whether I still have a relationship.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Then why did you?”\n\nGreg looked toward the road. “Because I thought if I left before you asked me to change, I could decide that the ending was mine.”\n\nThe anger shifted into something more painful.\n\n“I wasn’t asking you to stop being yourself,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“I was asking whether there was room for me in the future.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You keep saying you know.”\n\n“Because I do now.”\n\nI looked at him. “What did you figure out on the road?”\n\nHe rubbed a hand over his face. “I don’t know how to be asked to stay without hearing it as a demand to become someone else.”\n\n“And I don’t know how to ask for a future without hearing your silence as rejection.”\n\n“That’s a bad combination.”\n\n“It is.”\n\nThe night wind moved between us.\n\nGreg stepped closer. “I’m sorry.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“No. I need to say it better. I’m sorry I made my fear your problem. I’m sorry I left without telling you. I’m sorry I used distance to avoid a conversation I was supposed to have with you.”\n\nMy eyes burned.\n\n“I’m sorry I made it sound like you were failing because you wanted your life to stay recognizable,” I said. “I love the van. I love the way you make a home out of things other people overlook. I just need to know whether the home has room for me.”\n\nGreg's face crumpled into tenderness.\n\n“It does,” he said.\n\n“How do you know?”\n\n“Because when I drove away, every road looked wrong without you in the passenger seat.”\n\nI wanted to forgive him immediately. The wanting was not enough.\n\n“That’s romantic,” I said. “It’s not a plan.”\n\n“You’re right.”\n\nHe pulled a folded paper from his pocket. It was a receipt covered in handwriting.\n\n“I stopped at a diner and made a list,” he said. “Not a very good one.”\n\nI took it.\n\nThe list held small ideas. A better mattress. A second key. A savings account for repairs and emergencies. A way to spend part of each month in one place without treating the van like a prison. A promise to answer the phone when he needed space.\n\nAt the bottom, in larger letters, he had written: ASK, DO NOT RUN.\n\nI looked up.\n\n“I don’t need you to solve everything tonight,” I said.\n\n“Good. I’m very tired.”\n\n“I need you to come home.”\n\n“I’m here.”\n\n“I need you to stay long enough to talk tomorrow.”\n\n“I can do that.”\n\nI moved closer. “I need to know you’ll tell me before you leave next time.”\n\n“I will.”\n\n“And if you don’t?”\n\n“You get to be angry. You get to leave. You get to decide what you can live with.”\n\nThe answer was not a guarantee. It was accountability.\n\nI touched his cheek. “Can I kiss you?”\n\nHis eyes closed beneath my hand. “Yes.”\n\nThe kiss was not an eraser. It was an acknowledgment that love could survive a mistake without pretending the mistake had not happened.\n\nGreg held me carefully. When I pulled away, he kept his forehead against mine.\n\n“I love you,” he said.\n\n“I love you too.”\n\n“I’m going to say that before I do something stupid.”\n\n“You should try saying it before you drive for ten hours.”\n\n“That’s more advanced.”\n\nI laughed against his mouth.\n\nThe van was still parked beneath the trees. The road was still open behind it. Nothing had become magically safe.\n\nBut Greg had come back.\n\nThis time, he stayed.\n\nThe next day, Greg returned to the overlook with a notebook. He sat beneath the trees and wrote down the time he had left, the places he had stopped, and the moments when he could have called but did not.\n\n“You are documenting your crimes,” I said.\n\n“I am documenting my patterns.”\n\n“That sounds less dramatic.”\n\n“I can call it a crime if it helps me remember.”\n\nI sat beside him. “What do you want to do with the list?”\n\n“Show it to my therapist.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“And to you, if you want.”\n\n“I want the plan, not the punishment.”\n\nGreg nodded. “Then the plan is: when I need space, I say where I am going and when I will contact you. If I cannot talk, I send a message. If I break the plan, I tell you what happened instead of pretending it did not.”\n\n“And if I panic?”\n\n“You tell me you are panicking, not that I am evil.”\n\n“That is a high standard.”\n\n“I am willing to be ambitious.”\n\nI leaned into him. “I will try.”\n\n“Honestly?”\n\n“Honestly.”\n\nHe reached for my hand. The notebook lay open between us, the paper fluttering in the wind.\n\n“I don’t want to become the person who makes you monitor every silence,” Greg said.\n\n“Then keep coming back before silence becomes a story.”\n\n“I will.”\n\n“And I will tell you when I need reassurance.”\n\n“I will give it without making you apologize for needing it.”\n\nThe agreement was small. It did not guarantee perfection. It gave the future a direction.\n\nGreg closed the notebook and put it away.\n\n“Can I kiss you?” he asked.\n\n“You can.”\n\nHe kissed me beneath the trees. The road remained open behind the van, but neither of us needed to take it that day.\n\nWhen I returned to Beach City, Greg put the notebook in a drawer beside the new key.\n\n“You are not going to hide it?” I asked.\n\n“No. But I am not going to make it the center of our home.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nHe opened the drawer again. “Would you like to add anything?”\n\nI wrote one line beneath his: ASK BEFORE ASSUMING THE DOOR IS CLOSED FOREVER.\n\nGreg read it twice.\n\n“That is a good rule,” he said.\n\n“It is a difficult one.”\n\n“Most good rules are.”\n\nWe closed the drawer together.\n\nGreg did not open it again that night. Instead, he made dinner and let me choose the music.\n\nI chose something loud. He complained. I danced while he cooked.\n\n“You are distracting the chef,” he said.\n\n“The chef is very resilient.”\n\n“The chef is making soup.”\n\n“The soup will survive.”\n\nHe turned from the stove and kissed me. The argument became a memory before it could become a wall.\n\nLater, the notebook stayed closed in the drawer, and both of us slept.\n\nIn the morning, Greg opened the drawer only long enough to place the new key beside the notebook.\n\nThe two objects rested together without competing.\n\nIn the morning Greg held his phone over the breakfast table and stared at Steven's contact.\n\n“Something wrong?” I asked.\n\n“Nah. Well, kinda.” He turned the screen around. “When I get spooked, I disappear on everybody. Not just you. Steven knows the routine, and that doesn't make it fair.”\n\nI could hear how carefully he was trying not to make it sound like a speech.\n\n“You don't have to report every drive to your son.”\n\n“I know. I'm not gonna send him my location every time I need gas. I just think I owe him a way to tell the difference between taking space and vanishing.”\n\nHe typed a short message. *Had a rough day. I'm safe. No need to reply now. Hope you're doing okay.* Then he put the phone face down.\n\nHe didn't ask me whether the text proved he was a good father. He scraped the blackened edge off a pancake and slid the better half onto my plate.\n\n“Future-wise,” he said, “I don't want you thinking the van's a test of how much I love you. It has memories. It has a terrible suspension. It has the world's least comfortable drawer. I might want a house someday. I might want both.”\n\n“I don't need you to choose a building to prove you can stay.”\n\n“Great. Because I can't afford to replace that drawer.”\n\nI nudged his foot beneath the table. He caught it with his own, looking suddenly shy.\n\n“I also don't need to be the only place you call home,” he said. “I know you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. We're figuring out what ours looks like. I'm allowed to get scared without taking the wheel and driving away from the conversation.”\n\nI reached for his hand. His fingers were sticky with syrup, which was an extremely Greg way to make a declaration feel real.\n\n\nI made coffee. He made pancakes.\n\nNeither of us ran.\n\nThe road remained available, but home was closer.\n\nWe sat together on the couch until the light changed. Greg played a melody with one hand while I rested against his shoulder.\n\nIt was enough.\n\nFor now.\n\nThat was sufficient.\n\nThe plan could wait.","word_count":1810},"su-42":{"number":42,"title":"Folding Chairs and Honest Labor","content":"Greg had agreed to perform at a fundraiser before anyone told him there was no one assigned to put away the chairs.\n\nThat was how I found him at Starwake Pier, holding a box of tangled lantern cords in one hand and a folding table under his other arm.\n\n\"How's the glamorous life of a musician?\" I asked.\n\n\"Nobody warned me about furniture.\"\n\n\"Did you read the volunteer sheet?\"\n\n\"Yep.\"\n\n\"Did you put your own name next to every empty task?\"\n\nGreg glanced at the paper pinned to a post. Nearly every line had GREG written beside it.\n\n\"That is a wildly unfair accusation,\" he said. \"Some of those say 'Greg U.'\"\n\nHe was trying to make me laugh, which was ordinarily a successful strategy. I took the table from him instead.\n\n\"You're performing tonight. You also have sound checks, and you're supposed to make sure the speakers don't blow the fuse again. You can't do everything.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't sound convinced.\"\n\nGreg set the cords down.\n\n\"People need the money. The pier needs new supports. If somebody says they'll do a job and then doesn't, I can at least make sure it happens.\"\n\n\"I know why you do it. That doesn't make the plan workable.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. The sun caught on the edges of his hair.\n\n\"Do you have a better plan?\"\n\n\"I was hoping we could make one together.\"\n\nWe began with the chairs. There were forty-eight of them, three with loose hinges and one whose left leg did not quite touch the ground. I wrote the number at the top of the volunteer sheet and set the broken ones aside.\n\nGreg inspected the crooked chair.\n\n\"Hey, this one's got character.\"\n\n\"This one has a lawsuit.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThe fundraiser was meant to pay for safer pier railings and a storage shed so equipment wouldn't sit exposed to saltwater all winter. Greg had offered music. Mara had offered food. Tamsin had offered to oversee the repairs. Somewhere between the promises, nobody had agreed who would collect donations or clean up afterward.\n\nI borrowed a pencil from Pip and crossed GREG off the tasks he hadn't been asked to own.\n\n\"Somebody still has to do those,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes. Somebody can volunteer.\"\n\n\"What if they don't?\"\n\n\"Then we scale down the event. We don't pretend you owe free labor to everyone because you know how to play a guitar.\"\n\nHe sat on the edge of the stage. His expression softened, then tightened.\n\n\"Sometimes it feels like if I say no, the whole thing falls apart.\"\n\n\"Then the thing was badly designed.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That's a weirdly comforting insult to an event.\"\n\n\"One of my specialties.\"\n\nWe spent an hour asking people what they could actually do. Katara said her clinic volunteers could help with refreshments for an hour, not all night. Danse agreed to check the temporary stage railings but would not sign off on the damaged western section. Iroh could supervise tea and cleanup until dusk; after that, he had an appointment of his own.\n\nNola arrived for the sound check carrying a case of cables and a schedule that looked significantly less forgiving than Greg's hopeful volunteer sheet.\n\n\"Which of these jobs is paid?\" she asked.\n\nGreg read the paper. \"Uh. None of them?\"\n\n\"Then my crew's only doing the work in the contract. We have another booking tomorrow morning.\"\n\nShe wasn't being cruel. She was watching two sound technicians haul expensive equipment across a damp pier while half the volunteers argued about a banner.\n\nGreg scratched his beard. \"I was going to do the teardown myself.\"\n\n\"You can't disconnect a stage you're still playing on,\" Nola said.\n\n\"Well, when you put it that way.\"\n\nMaui appeared carrying the marked stack of chairs. \"Fear not! The great Maui can clear the entire pier with one hand.\"\n\n\"Please use both,\" Tamsin called from the railing. \"And don't stack them in the exit.\"\n\nMaui looked as though somebody had insulted the artistry of his shoulders. Greg grinned and tried a little drumroll on a chair seat. The chair squealed.\n\n\"Don't give him percussion,\" I said.\n\nNola checked the booking sheet. She moved the sound test earlier and made a separate line for the load-out crew. Danse could check the approved stage railings, but the electrical load would still have to be signed off by the responsible technician.\n\nGreg listened to the new timing. \"So two sets, no mystery third set, and I don't become a chair company afterward.\"\n\n\"Correct,\" Nola said.\n\nHe hummed a rising four-note phrase. It sounded like relief.\n\nMaui stopped beside us. \"Where's my name on the program?\"\n\n\"Under chairs,\" I told him.\n\nHe sighed. \"You people refuse to recognize an artist.\"\n\nMaui offered to carry every chair personally.\n\nI handed him the stack labeled FIFTEEN CHAIRS, TWO PEOPLE.\n\nHe read it.\n\n\"You're limiting my heroism.\"\n\n\"I'm improving its scheduling.\"\n\nThe sound check introduced a new problem.\n\nGreg's amplifier buzzed when the stage crew switched on the lantern string. Danse watched the voltage meter and asked to shut the shared circuit down until they could identify the load.\n\n“Hey, no argument here,” Greg said. “I've already had one amp go out with a pop. Not doing that on purpose.”\n\nMaui appeared beside them with a coil of cable balanced on his hook. “I can hang the lanterns from the tallest post. Boom. Problem solved.”\n\n“Problem relocated,” Danse corrected. “The post hasn't been inspected for that load.”\n\nMaui looked offended. “I've lifted islands.”\n\n“And we appreciate the resume,” Greg said. “But the island probably didn't have a building inspector.”\n\nI laughed; Maui did not. He put the cable down and asked how he could help without making the power problem worse. Nola directed him to the approved string of unlit lanterns at the back of the square. He carried them with the grave solemnity of someone demonstrating world-shaking restraint.\n\nGreg checked the amplifier's cable, turned the gain down, and waited while the qualified technician tested a separate circuit. The replacement power lead didn't reach as far as the original, so he agreed to a smaller stage area and changed the set list to suit the room.\n\n“You okay with being less loud?” I asked him.\n\n“Less loud?” he said, aghast. “Jasper, I can make an acoustic guitar sound embarrassing all by itself.”\n\nDanse recorded the permitted load. Maui came back carrying the folding chairs he'd been assigned. They both looked pleased with work that hadn't required a dramatic announcement.\n\nIt wasn't a contest over who could save the concert. People had come to hear music and raise money for repairs. We were trying not to give them another repair bill.\n\n\nAt noon we had six volunteers, a safe seating plan, and a stage that did not require anyone to stand on the broken section. Greg looked at the sheet, then at me.\n\n\"You know the terrible thing?\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"It works.\"\n\n\"I do hate it when that happens.\"\n\nThe first guests arrived while Greg tuned his guitar. Some came from Beach City; others wandered over from the Lanternwell Square market. A child asked whether all the songs would be about doors. Greg promised at least one about chairs, which earned me a look from Mara that could have curdled milk.\n\nA man wearing a wide brown hat stopped beside the stage.\n\n\"You're Greg Universe, right?\"\n\n\"That's me.\"\n\n\"Great! Heard you were playing all night. Can you do another three songs after the fundraiser? My kid's a huge fan.\"\n\nGreg smiled out of reflex.\n\n\"Uh, maybe—\"\n\nI watched the word forming.\n\nThen he stopped.\n\n\"Actually,\" he said, \"I've agreed to two sets. I can sign the kid's program afterward if they want, but I've got to help with the equipment before heading home.\"\n\nThe man blinked.\n\n\"You're not doing an encore?\"\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\nFor a terrible second, I thought the man might argue. Instead, he shrugged and went to find seats.\n\nGreg turned toward me, looking almost surprised.\n\n\"That was uncomfortable.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And the sky did not fall.\"\n\n\"Good. We're running low on replacement sky.\"\n\nHe laughed, then reached for my hand. His fingers were rough against my palm from the guitar strings.\n\n\"You okay with me learning this slowly?\" he asked.\n\n\"I'd be suspicious if you learned it all at once.\"\n\nThe concert went well enough to feel like a real concert instead of a miracle. One microphone buzzed. The first song began a little late because a child dropped a cup in the front row. The audience sang the chorus of Greg's old road song louder than he did.\n\nHalfway through the second set, a light on the pier flickered.\n\nDanse stood at once.\n\nGreg stopped playing.\n\n\"Problem?\"\n\n\"Power fluctuation. Keep everyone off the western platform.\"\n\nThere was no panic. We had actually rehearsed what to do.\n\nMaui helped move chairs to the safer end. Katara brought the children to the square. Greg put down his guitar and checked the speaker plugs while Danse tested the line. They found a loose connection and decided not to restart the stage circuit.\n\nThe remaining songs would be acoustic.\n\n\"I can do acoustic,\" Greg announced to the audience. \"It's mostly what I do anyway.\"\n\nSomeone cheered.\n\nThe night finished with a small circle of people and no dramatic lighting. I preferred it. Greg's voice carried across the water, catching in the warm space between the lanterns. I could see how tired he was, and how happy he was that he hadn't spent the whole evening trying to be four people at once.\n\nWhen the final note faded, he looked at me over the strings.\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe smiled back.\n\nThen he stood up and helped fold the first chair.\n\n\"You're not allowed to do all of them,\" I reminded him.\n\n\"Just this one.\"\n\n\"Fine.\"\n\nWe stacked them in pairs, carried equipment to the dry storeroom, and counted the donation box with Mara present. The fundraiser would cover the railings, not the new shed. That was less than we'd hoped. It was enough to begin.\n\nThe last guest left with a paper cup of tea and the promise that the next event would have a proper cleanup roster.\n\nGreg and I walked toward the Starwake Door after midnight.\n\nHe was carrying only his guitar. I was carrying the repaired volunteer sheet, folded small enough to fit in my pocket.\n\n\"Good night?\" I asked.\n\n\"Best one I've had in a while.\"\n\n\"Even without an encore?\"\n\n\"Especially without one.\"\n\nHe tucked a loose strand of hair behind my ear. The touch was gentle, a question rather than a claim.\n\n\"Can I give you one private encore?\"\n\n\"Depends. Is it about chairs?\"\n\n\"No promises.\"\n\nI kissed him before he could invent a second verse.\n\nLater, when the van door closed behind us, we left the music and the rest of the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning, Greg found the volunteer sheet on the dashboard. Under my handwriting he'd added a new rule:\n\nGREG IS ALLOWED TO BE A MUSICIAN, NOT A FURNITURE COMPANY.\n\nI added one more line beneath it.\n\nJASPER IS NOT THE ENTIRE MANAGEMENT DEPARTMENT EITHER.\n\nHe kissed my cheek while I wrote.\n\nWe both liked the amended version.","word_count":1873}},"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-08":{"number":8,"title":"What the Door Kept","content":"The Threadway beneath Lanternwell opened for my key on the first cold morning of the season.\n\nNeri found me standing before it, watching a pale rectangle of light reveal the shape of an ordinary room. Inside was a desk, a chair, a stack of letters, and the faded pattern of a curtain I suddenly recognized.\n\nMy hands went numb.\n\n“That's where I came from,” I said.\n\nNeri nodded. “It is one place you've been.”\n\nNot the place I belonged. Not the place I had to return to. One place.\n\nNeri held the key ring out to me instead of turning the lock on my behalf.\n\n“Before you go in,” they said, “there are three things the archive needs to be clear about. First, the room is a private record, not a public exhibition. Second, I will not copy a letter you haven't chosen to read. Third, closing the Threadway does not mean closing it forever.”\n\n“Did you know what was in there?”\n\n“No. I was given the room's location, not its contents.”\n\nThe difference mattered. A map might show a door without telling somebody what it would mean to stand inside it. I wanted to be able to come back without finding my old life arranged into an archive display because someone else believed memory should be useful.\n\nA figure appeared at the far end of the corridor. Maui, enormous and unmistakably trying to look casual, had a basket of sea bread hooked over one arm. Mini Maui leaned from his shoulder and pointed at the open room. Maui took a breath as though ready to announce he'd found the perfect way to help.\n\n“Would it be heroic,” he began, “if I—”\n\n“No,” I said.\n\nHe shut his mouth. Mini Maui pretended to fall over dead from the shock.\n\n“Okay,” Maui replied after a moment. “I'm going to wait where you can see me, but not where I can see in. Very boring heroism. Nobody makes statues of it.”\n\nNeri didn't smile until he had stepped aside. Then they wrote the crossing in the log: entry permitted to the traveler, no contents recorded. The line was small. Its importance wasn't.\n\nI kept the key in my own hand.\n\n\nI asked for time alone.\n\nBeyond the doorway, memory returned with a kind of terrible precision. I saw myself keeping lists for other people, making promises too quickly, laughing when I wanted to say no because laughing was easier than disappointing anyone. I remembered warmth too: a favorite book, rain on the windows, the thrill of building something that worked. My old life had not been entirely misery. That made the choice harder.\n\nThere were people I had loved, even if I couldn't yet recall their faces. There were obligations that belonged to me and others I had accepted because nobody asked whether I wanted them.\n\nThe door did not tell me which was which.\n\nI sat on the floor and cried. Not because the room was terrible, but because it was familiar enough to hurt.\n\nThe room contained evidence of a life I had made, but not a verdict on who I was.\n\nA small pile of mended clothes sat in the drawer beneath the desk. The needlework was uneven. I touched a seam with my thumb and remembered sitting at that desk one rainy evening, sewing too quickly because I had promised the shirt back by morning. I remembered the relief when nobody asked why I'd made the promise in the first place.\n\nBeside the letters was a list of repairs. Every task had a date and a box. Some had been crossed out; others had been copied again on new pages. At the bottom, in handwriting that was unmistakably mine, I had written: *Do it properly or don't do it at all.*\n\nI sank into the chair.\n\nThe sentence sounded like the voice that followed me when I fixed the bridge lanterns. I did not know whether it belonged to someone who had hurt me or to a rule I'd made to survive an impossible week. The difference mattered. I couldn't tell from a page.\n\nI opened one envelope. It contained a thank-you note for helping someone move furniture, written by a person whose name I didn't yet recognize. The note was kind. It did not say I owed them anything.\n\nThat hurt differently.\n\nI had been expecting a past built entirely from bad choices. Instead I found evidence that people had sometimes loved me without demanding labor in return. I had also found the old habit of making each kindness into another task.\n\nI put the letter back.\n\nThrough the curtain I could see a narrow strip of sky, the kind of grey that means rain is nearby. The window latch stuck when I tried to open it. My hand knew precisely how to lift the left corner and turn the catch. For a moment the ease of it made me dizzy.\n\nA memory did not have to be a command.\n\nI could remember the latch without moving back into this room. I could keep the thank-you letter without promising to be available to its author forever. I could return another day, or not.\n\nI went to the threshold and found Neri waiting on the far side of the hall, looking at a book to give me privacy.\n\n\"Can I leave it as it is?\" I asked.\n\n\"The room?\"\n\n\"Everything. I don't want someone sorting it while I'm gone.\"\n\nNeri shut the book. \"We can mark the room as private and preserve it. If you later want help cataloging your own things, you can ask.\"\n\n\"Would I have to explain what I'm keeping?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThe word was wonderfully uncomplicated.\n\nI took one small notebook from the desk, not the whole pile. Then I closed the drawer and returned the key to my pocket.\n\n\"I don't want to decide my whole life today,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then don't.\"\n\nIt was almost funny, how much relief fit inside two ordinary words.\n\nAt the doorway I stopped long enough to examine the hinges. The metal was ordinary, worn by years of use. Nothing about it announced that entering would bind me to the place again.\n\n\"Will it stay here if I leave?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"The door may close between crossings. The room does not vanish because you choose not to enter it.\"\n\nI thought of the letters on the desk. I hadn't read them all. Some were addressed to me, others to people whose names still meant nothing. One envelope had been opened and resealed so many times that the corner had gone soft.\n\n\"I don't want you to sort those for me,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded. \"Then I won't.\"\n\nWe wrote down what I was taking: one notebook, the little brass key, and the memory of a room that had once held both good days and bad ones. The letters could stay in place until I decided whether I wanted to read them, contact their writers, or ask for help understanding them.\n\nI stood in the corridor until the shaking in my hands eased. When I was ready, I walked back through the Threadway.\n\nWhen I returned to Lanternwell, Maui was draped over a bench with the melodramatic misery of a demigod specifically forbidden to perform a rescue. He'd brought enough food for five people and left it within my reach. A gull was stalking the bread. Maui glared at it like he'd finally found an opponent worthy of his reputation.\n\nHe rose too quickly when he saw me, nearly sending a basket of fruit rolling down the path.\n\n\"Good news,\" he announced. \"I have heroically refrained from breaking into the mysterious room, turning into a bird to peek through the window, or interrogating the ancient door about its secrets.\"\n\n\"That's quite a list.\"\n\n\"Terrible afternoon. Very little glory.\"\n\nMini Maui punched the air in triumph and immediately pointed toward the fruit. Maui looked at the banana he'd nearly crushed in his fist, then offered it to me with an embarrassed flourish.\n\n\"I was going to make a grand speech about whatever you found,\" he admitted. \"Didn't know whether you'd want one. So I brought lunch instead. Far less impressive.\"\n\n\"Much more useful.\"\n\nHe brightened. \"I knew it! The great Maui, champion of lunch.\"\n\nI took the fruit. He didn't ask me to tell him what had happened behind the door. The restraint was real; so was the loud, ridiculous man performing it.\n\n“Did you remember?” he asked.\n\n“Some of it.”\n\n“Are you going back?”\n\n“Maybe, someday. There are things I need to understand. People I may want to find. But I don't want to go back just because I know the way.”\n\nHe didn't try to tell me what to do.\n\n“I was afraid you'd think I'd become somebody else,” I admitted.\n\nHis laugh was gentle. “Jasper, I'm a shapeshifter. I would be a spectacular hypocrite.”\n\n“I'm serious.”\n\n“So am I.” He crouched beside me. “I liked you when you couldn't tell me your name. I like you now that you can tell me when I'm being an idiot. I'm not measuring you against a stranger in a room I've never seen.”\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n“What if I find out I wasn't very brave?”\n\n“Then we start with the brave thing you did today.”\n\n“What was that?”\n\n“You came back because you wanted to, not because I asked.”\n\nThe door behind us closed quietly. My key did not vanish. The choice would remain available.\n\nNeri met us before dusk with a folded paper and no attempt to ask what I had seen.\n\n“The room is recorded as a voluntary return route,” they said. “That means you may use it. It doesn't mean we can publish your memories.”\n\nMaui looked affronted. “Obviously. I'm a master of respecting secrets. I've kept at least four just this afternoon.”\n\nMini Maui shook its head.\n\nNeri explained that the crossing register recorded whether a doorway was safe to use, not what travelers thought or whom they planned to visit. I could ask for an escort without surrendering my whole history.\n\nMaui opened his mouth, ready to volunteer to carry me through the door next time. Then he stopped.\n\n“You can ask,” I said.\n\n“Fine. Would you like the great Maui's spectacularly voluntary assistance?”\n\n“Not today.”\n\nHe sighed. “A whole day of heroic restraint. Nobody sings about the hard stuff.”\n\nNeri let me decide whether to keep the paper. I did. Memory had given me facts; these people were giving me choices that survived uncertainty.\n\nWhen I asked Maui to carry the dinner basket, he grinned as if I'd entrusted him with a legendary quest. He did not pretend it was the same thing as deciding my future.\n\n\nThat night I opened the notebook I'd begun in the bakery room. On one page I wrote the things I wanted to remember: the taste of bread, the sound of rain, the satisfaction of repairing a lantern, the way the sea changed color at dusk.\n\nOn the next page I wrote the things I wanted to learn: how to sail better, how to say no without an apology, how to return to an old place without becoming an old fear.\n\nMaui looked over the list and pointed at the final blank line.\n\n“Forgot one.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“How to win an argument with a demigod.”\n\nI picked up my pencil.\n\n“I thought I'd already mastered that.”\n\n“Outrageous.”\n\nI wrote it down anyway.","word_count":1916},"maui-18":{"number":18,"title":"One Door Closed by Choice","content":"The door to my old room was still there.\n\nI had avoided it for three days after the Lantern Walk, which was an impressive achievement considering it stood only a few turns from the square. It was an ordinary painted door set into a stretch of old stone. The Threadway did not advertise where it led. Only the small brass plate with my chosen name marked it as mine.\n\nMaui stopped beside me.\n\n\"Do you want me to come?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could almost hear the magnificent speech he was about to give about his usefulness. Then he shut it and took one theatrical step aside. \"Fine. I shall guard this extremely dangerous patch of hallway against dust and suspicious furniture. You're welcome.\"\n\nI reached for the handle.\n\nThe brass was cold. When I turned it, a familiar smell met me: laundry soap, paper, and the faint dust of a room that had not been lived in for a while. The desk was where I remembered it. A book lay facedown beside a cup. The light came through the window at an angle I recognized without knowing why.\n\nThen I noticed the notebook.\n\nIt sat beneath the lamp, bound in faded green cloth, with a ribbon I could have tied myself. I knew the handwriting on the first page before I read the words.\n\nI had written a list of things I was afraid to ask for.\n\nThe first was simple: a day when I didn't have to explain myself.\n\nThe second had been crossed out so hard that the paper was nearly torn.\n\nThe third said: Don't let people decide where I belong just because they remember more than I do.\n\nI sat on the edge of the bed.\n\nI had expected the room to give me answers. Instead it gave me evidence of a person who had been frightened long before Lanternweald.\n\nFootsteps sounded in the hall behind me.\n\nMaui did not come through the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\" he called.\n\n\"I'm here.\"\n\n\"Are you all right?\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\nThere was a long pause.\n\n\"Do you want me to wait?\"\n\n\"Yes. Outside.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\nI heard him move away from the doorway. Not far, but far enough to give me space.\n\nThat was harder than rescue. I knew it was harder for him, because it was hard for me too.\n\nI stayed in the room for another twenty minutes. I opened the desk drawer and found a broken pen, two bus tickets, and a photograph turned facedown. I did not turn it over. I wasn't ready to look at a face I might not recognize.\n\nOne ticket was creased nearly through the printed stop. The dates were different. I tried to remember whether either journey had been exciting or miserable, and my mind gave me nothing.\n\nThe silence was worse than a bad memory in some ways. There was no person to argue with. Just a scrap of paper proving I'd been somewhere without telling me why.\n\nMy fingers hovered over the photograph.\n\n\"Not today,\" I said aloud.\n\nI was talking to furniture now. Wonderful. I asked the desk whether it objected to the drawer remaining closed. It offered no opinion.\n\nA thud came through the wall, followed by a muttered complaint. Maui had almost certainly collided with something. I smiled before I meant to.\n\nI could love the man waiting outside without handing him every missing part of myself. I could keep the photograph facedown and still choose to return another day.\n\nOne of the drawer handles was loose. I wrote it in the notebook as a thing to repair when I was ready. Maui would undoubtedly offer to carry the entire desk away and call it a rescue. I'd tell him no. He'd make a scene. And then, if he was learning what I thought he was learning, he'd let me find a screwdriver.\n\nFor the first time since opening the room, the thought of tomorrow didn't feel entirely like a threat.\n\nInstead I wrote the date in the notebook.\n\nI live somewhere else now.\n\nThe words looked absurdly small.\n\nI added: That does not mean this room has stopped being mine.\n\nWhen I finally stepped into the hall, Maui was sitting on the floor with his back against the wall. He had taken the opportunity to carve a tiny fish into the handle of his walking stick.\n\nThe wooden fish was magnificent only by Maui's standards: one eye sat higher than the other, and its tail looked like a badly folded map.\n\n\"I was going to turn into a gull and perform a dramatic patrol of the roof,\" he admitted. \"Then I remembered you said you wanted time alone. Very inconsiderate of you, frankly. Do you know how entertaining a gull patrol would have been?\"\n\n\"Was that hard?\"\n\n\"Unbelievably. I have a whole hook and nothing to do with it.\"\n\nMini Maui popped onto his shoulder and gave him a congratulatory pat, then fell over laughing at the fish carving.\n\nMaui glared at his own skin. \"You try carving without a proper knife.\"\n\nHe wanted to ask me everything about the room. The questions crowded his face. Instead he held up the lopsided fish.\n\n\"Present for the heroic survivor of a very boring afternoon.\"\n\nI took it. He looked so pleased that I nearly forgot how frightened I'd been behind the door. Nearly. The fear was still mine, and he was waiting to see what I would share. He'd earned that place beside me by not turning a private crossing into a show.\n\n\"You waited,\" I said.\n\n\"You asked.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd get bored.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\nHe held up the fish.\n\n\"I gave boredom a face.\"\n\nIt was a much better fish than the one on the broom handle.\n\nI laughed.\n\nThen I cried.\n\nThe tears surprised both of us. Maui stood, but he didn't reach for me until I stepped into his arms. His coat smelled of sea air and the smoke from Mara's bakery. I pressed my face against his shoulder and let the feelings come without arranging them into a story.\n\nAfter a while he asked, \"Do you want to go back in?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you want to close the door?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI turned, took the handle, and pulled it shut.\n\nThe latch clicked. Nothing in the arch dimmed. No bell sounded in protest. The Threadway did not punish me for leaving a room I wasn't ready to inhabit.\n\nI stood there for a moment, breathing.\n\n\"Is this running away?\" I asked.\n\nMaui considered the door.\n\n\"I don't think so.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you know where it is. And you're deciding when to return.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"That was a surprisingly good answer.\"\n\nHe grinned faintly.\n\n\"I'll try not to make a habit of it.\"\n\nWe walked back toward Tideglass in silence. Lanternwell Square smelled of bread and damp leaves. The frost was melting from the roofs. I noticed that the lantern I'd painted was still hanging beside the signal post, its wick extinguished and the circle of amber paint intact.\n\nBefore we reached home, I turned back toward the lantern by the signal post. The glass was misted from the cold. Somebody had replaced the wick while I was away, and the flame inside was steadier than the one I'd left.\n\n\"Who fixed it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Mara mentioned a volunteer on the morning shift,\" Maui said. \"They wrote it in the little service book.\"\n\nI found the book beneath the sheltered ledge. My earlier repair was still recorded there, along with the name of the person who had checked it today. The place didn't stop working when I stepped through a door.\n\n\"That's a strange thing to be relieved about,\" I said.\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"I suppose I thought everybody would be waiting for me to tell them what happened.\"\n\nMaui looked genuinely puzzled. \"We wanted you safe. That's not the same as wanting a report.\"\n\nThe words struck harder than I expected. I had a green notebook filled with memories of being useful, and somewhere in it a list I'd been afraid to show anyone. I didn't want those old obligations to walk out of the room with me and start issuing new orders.\n\nAt the house, I set my boots by the stove and looked at the fish carving Maui had left there. Its tail was uneven. He'd spent the afternoon sanding it rather than trying to follow me through the doorway.\n\n\"Can we have dinner before we talk?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if I only tell you half of it?\"\n\n\"Then I'll know half.\"\n\n\"You're becoming dangerously sensible.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n\nHe set out bread and warmed soup while I changed into dry clothes. Nothing about the food was magnificent, but I found myself hungry for the first time all day. When I returned, he had pulled out the chair I liked without making a ceremony of it.\n\nI took his hand briefly before we ate.\n\nI didn't owe him every word in the green notebook to be worthy of his company. I could choose what to share and still choose to stay.\n\nAt home, I put the green notebook on the kitchen table.\n\nMaui set down the fish carving beside it.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what you found?\" he asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"A list I wrote before I came here. Things I was afraid to ask for.\"\n\nHe sat opposite me.\n\n\"What did you ask for?\"\n\n\"Mostly nothing.\"\n\nHe winced.\n\n\"That's not very you.\"\n\n\"It was me. Maybe it still is, sometimes.\"\n\nHe thought about that.\n\n\"I've been trying to learn how to ask instead of deciding for people,\" he said. \"Maybe you're learning the same thing from the other side.\"\n\nI looked at the notebook.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nI turned the page and wrote a new list. Not demands. Wishes.\n\nI want to visit the old room again when I choose.\n\nI want Maui to stay tonight.\n\nI want room for other people I love without making anyone a substitute.\n\nI want to know what the bell means, but not at the cost of my right to say no.\n\nI read the list aloud.\n\nMaui listened.\n\nWhen I finished, he said, \"I can say yes to one of those right now.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"Staying.\"\n\nI felt heat rise in my face.\n\n\"You don't have to be so pleased with yourself.\"\n\n\"Impossible.\"\n\nHe reached for my hand across the table.\n\n\"Do you want company because you're sad,\" he asked, \"or because you want me?\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"Both. Is that allowed?\"\n\nHe smiled, but there was no triumph in it.\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nI went around the table and sat beside him. He kissed me softly, then paused when I caught his collar and drew him closer. The room was warm from the little fire. Outside, the sea moved in steady breaths along the stones.\n\nWe talked for another hour, about the old notebook and the strange relief of leaving something unfinished. We laughed about his carved fish, which had a suspicious resemblance to Fen.\n\nWhen I told him I wanted to go upstairs, he asked once more whether I was sure.\n\n\"I am.\"\n\nWe left the notebook on the table and closed the bedroom door. The rest of the night belonged to us, with no demand to make grief beautiful.\n\nLater, when the fire had burned low, Maui noticed the open corner of my notebook beneath a paperweight. He didn't touch it.\n\n“I keep wondering what you wrote,” he admitted.\n\n“You can ask.”\n\n“Okay. What did you write?”\n\n“Things I don't want to forget. Not all of them are things I want to tell you yet.”\n\nHis mouth twisted with disappointment. He tried to cover it by striking a pose from the edge of the couch.\n\n“The great Maui, defeated by a notebook. They'll put it in the songs.”\n\n“Only if the notebook agrees.”\n\nMini Maui climbed along his arm and made an elaborate show of guarding a tiny imaginary lock. I laughed. The joke gave us a little room.\n\nMaui sat again, less theatrically this time. “I had people sing what they wanted me to be. Sometimes the songs made me brave. Sometimes they made it impossible to admit I was tired.”\n\n“Moana didn't ask you to be invincible,” I said.\n\n“No,” he answered. “She asked me to steer the boat when it mattered. Much harder to brag about steering.”\n\nI rested my hand against his wrist. He looked at it as though he were waiting for permission from a far more important authority than the sea.\n\n“You can stay beside me without knowing every page,” I told him.\n\n“I'm getting that.”\n\n“Slowly.”\n\n“Hey. Stone takes a while to carve. You want something good, you put in the work.”\n\nHe tilted his forehead against mine. His hair brushed my cheek, soft and warm, and I felt his fingers close around mine. The bedroom door was already shut. Nothing we wanted from each other required me to tell him the parts of my history I wasn't ready to share.\n\nHe fell asleep with one hand open near the notebook. I left it where it was, and reached for him instead.\n\n\nIn the morning, the notebook was still there beside the little wooden fish.\n\nI opened to the page I'd written.\n\nUnder the list, Maui had added a sentence in enormous handwriting.\n\nYOU CAN ASK ME AGAIN TOMORROW.\n\nI laughed so hard I nearly spilled my tea.\n\nThen I wrote beneath it, in much smaller letters:\n\nI probably will.","word_count":2273},"maui-42":{"number":42,"title":"Where the Water Cannot Go","content":"Fen spread three maps on the table and weighted them down with stones.\n\nThe oldest showed a blue line running beneath the square toward Tideglass. The second, drawn after the Closed Bells, ended that line in thick black ink. The third was ours, covered in flood marks, inspection dates, and the small angry circles Tamsin drew wherever a repair still needed money.\n\n\"Water doesn't disappear because somebody closes a door,\" Fen said.\n\nToph tapped the table.\n\n\"Neither do the consequences.\"\n\nI stood beside Maui, watching Fen trace the old route.\n\nBefore the sealing, the passage had carried excess rainwater away from the terraces and floodplain into a controlled coastal outlet during certain tide conditions. It wasn't a miracle. It had gates, inspections, and periods when it couldn't be used safely. After the lower barrier was sealed, the valley had relied on smaller surface drains and a series of improvised diversions.\n\nThe diversions worked in ordinary weather.\n\nIn storms, they pushed water toward the same homes and gardens again and again.\n\nSela pointed to the map.\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\nThen another point.\n\n\"Mine too. I thought the floods were just getting worse.\"\n\n\"They may be,\" Fen said. \"But the blocked route has made the pattern less forgiving.\"\n\nNeri sat at the far end of the table with a stack of copied maintenance receipts. Their face was pale.\n\n\"The council kept repairing the surface channels,\" they said. \"We told ourselves we were improving the valley.\"\n\n\"You were repairing symptoms,\" Tamsin answered. \"Sometimes repairs were necessary. They weren't the whole plan.\"\n\nI felt a cold ache beneath my ribs.\n\nThe old decision had not ended when the keepers died or the records burned. It had become a pattern in the landscape, the kind people accepted because they had lived with it long enough.\n\nMaui stared at the map.\n\n\"There,\" he said, tapping the thick black line. \"Open it. Let the water go where it's trying to go.\"\n\nFen shot him a look. \"The last time somebody thought that, the passage closed on people.\"\n\n\"I know.\" His voice rose. \"That's why I want it open. Nobody should have to watch their house flood because someone a hundred years ago was afraid!\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest, lifted a tiny imaginary gate, and immediately fell backward under the weight. Maui looked down and made an irritated sound.\n\n\"Great. Even my tattoos have joined the committee.\"\n\nToph put a hand on the table. \"You can lift a rock, big guy. You can't tell me whether the soil under it will hold.\"\n\nMaui's mouth opened. For a moment I expected another argument about his legendary strength. Then he stared at the garden marked beyond the line and shut it again.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what you *can* check,\" he said to Fen. \"And then give me something heavy to do that won't make it worse.\"\n\nThe demand was still loud. The choice to listen was real. They could exist in the same sentence without erasing each other.\n\n\"Then open the barrier.\"\n\nFen shook their head.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"People are getting wet.\"\n\n\"And they'd get much wetter if an unstable tunnel failed under the spring.\"\n\nThe silence was uncomfortable.\n\nDanse laid out the inspection results from the upper chamber. The mortar was different in two sections. One support showed long-term movement. We could test the surface outlet and keep the temporary channels open, but the lower route required engineering work before any attempt at full reopening.\n\nI wanted to argue.\n\nInstead I asked what the current closure actually meant.\n\n\"The upper maintenance hatch can be opened under supervision for inspection,\" Tamsin said. \"The lower flood barrier remains closed. Surface drains need additional capacity before the next storm.\"\n\nEdda raised her hand.\n\n\"And the detour must be usable by people who can't take the steps to the upper ridge.\"\n\nWe rewrote the work list.\n\nThe council would fund an accessible temporary path and a monitored surface overflow. Tideglass would provide tide data, Riverstone would oversee drainage effects on gardens, Brasswatch would test the portable pump and new fittings, and Moonroot would preserve the old plans without claiming they gave anyone permission to enter unsafe spaces.\n\nThe work needed money and patience.\n\nNeither was popular.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri by the fountain.\n\nThey were staring at the old stone arch.\n\n\"I used to think the sealed route was part of our history,\" they said. \"Something unfortunate and finished.\"\n\n\"It wasn't finished.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\n\"Do you hate me for not asking sooner?\"\n\nI had to think before answering.\n\n\"I'm angry that people kept suffering. I'm glad you're asking now. Both are true.\"\n\nThey nodded slowly.\n\n\"That is more than I expected.\"\n\nThe temporary channel was built over the following week. It ran along the safe upper path, using removable sections that could be cleaned without entering the old tunnel. Sela helped place the runoff markers near the garden. Edda tested the detour in wet weather. Toph checked the supports while Tamsin logged the movement.\n\nMaui carried materials when asked and learned the names of three workers who had previously been invisible to him. He brought hot food from the pantry after the late shift, then stayed to wash the pots.\n\nHalfway through installing the last removable section, the tide turned sooner than Fen had predicted. Water pressed against the temporary barrier and the nearest joint began to shiver.\n\nMaui planted his feet and caught the side beam before it could shift.\n\n“I've got it!” he shouted.\n\nToph felt the movement under her boots. “You have the beam. You don't have the foundation, Muscles.”\n\nEdda had already called for the people on the lower walk to move behind the marked line. Fen checked the tide gauge and told the crew to stop feeding more water through the test section.\n\n“Let me lift it,” Maui offered. “I can move the whole thing onto higher ground.”\n\nTamsin looked at the bolts he was holding. “If you lift now, you'll tear out the anchors on the other end.”\n\nFor an instant he seemed ready to argue. His hook flashed in his hand. I knew the kind of story he liked: one enormous effort, a magnificent rescue, cheers and a place beside the fire where everyone would repeat what he'd done.\n\nThen he lowered the hook.\n\n“Tell me what to hold,” he said.\n\nEdda directed him to support one beam while the workers released pressure from the inspection gate. Toph checked the ground, Tamsin resecured the movable brackets and Fen recorded the tide height at which the test had failed. I held the tools, dry enough to use, and tried not to think about how close the water had come to soaking the market stalls.\n\nWhen the barrier stopped moving, nobody cheered. The crew had to revise the safe operating range and postpone opening the upper route during high tide. A closed gate was frustrating. It was better than a false promise.\n\nMaui wiped saltwater from his forehead. “Well,” he said, “I only saved one beam.”\n\n“You listened to three people while saving it,” Toph said. “That's your new record.”\n\nHe looked offended for a moment. Then he grinned. “Someone write that down. I want it on a monument.”\n\nTamsin handed him the inspection sheet. “Sign here instead.”\n\nHe did, beneath the workers whose measurements had actually kept the channel standing.\n\n\nThe work did not make him smaller.\n\nI was beginning to think it made him more himself.\n\nOn the final test day, a controlled flow of water ran through the temporary channel and out toward the coast. Fen watched the gauges. Danse checked the pump. Sela stood beside the garden, waiting to see whether her soil would flood again.\n\nThe first test passed.\n\nThe second revealed a small leak near the lower joint.\n\nWe stopped and repaired it.\n\nThe third passed within the limits the team had written down.\n\nNobody cheered until Fen said the words.\n\n\"Safe for the posted conditions.\"\n\nThen we cheered.\n\nSela insisted on walking the team through the garden once the test ended. The new flow had missed the lowest bed, but a narrow strip of soil beside the path was damp in a way it hadn't been before.\n\n\"I want that measured tomorrow,\" she said. \"It may be nothing. My seedlings don't care whether we call it nothing.\"\n\nFen agreed and added the observation to the test sheet. Tamsin marked the difference between water that had been deliberately released during the controlled trial and rain they couldn't predict. Edda asked whether the temporary channel would still leave an accessible route if one section had to be removed for cleaning.\n\n\"We can test that,\" Danse answered.\n\n\"Before the next storm,\" she said.\n\nToph rested her feet against the path and made a face. \"At least the stone isn't shifting.\"\n\n\"Not shifting under the conditions we measured,\" Tamsin corrected.\n\nToph laughed. \"You engineers are contagious.\"\n\nMaui watched the exchange without offering to move the entire channel. I was absurdly proud of him.\n\nWhen the work stopped for a break, he sat beside me on a low wall.\n\n\"I was THIS close to announcing that I saved the entire valley,\" he admitted, spreading his arms. \"Then Sela mentioned her seedlings and Fen mentioned the gauges. Nobody understands the hard life of a humble legend.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Is that wrong?\"\n\n\"No. It's a thing we want, not a fact we can claim yet.\"\n\nHe looked out toward Tideglass. \"You make hope sound like paperwork.\"\n\n\"Sometimes hope needs a place to put the numbers.\"\n\nHe smiled at that, and when I leaned against him his arm settled around me without pulling me away from the garden. We could care about the place and each other at the same time.\n\nI thought of the old gate beneath the Closed Bells line. It remained sealed for reasons we were still trying to understand. We didn't have to open it to prove the work we'd done mattered. For now, the new surface route had passed a limited test, and people who lived beside it had the right to question what came next.\n\nThe cheering stopped as soon as Fen asked us to record the test conditions.\n\n\"If the tide rises past the marked line, close the channel. If the debris screen blocks, close it until it's cleared. If nobody on duty answers, don't improvise.\"\n\nMaui rested on his shovel. \"You have an extraordinary talent for ruining celebrations.\"\n\n\"And keeping people alive for the next one,\" Fen answered.\n\nSela asked about frozen winter ground. Fen admitted the team had no winter data. Rather than declare permanent success, they wrote down what needed monitoring. Edda tested the route in the evening light and pointed out a marker that disappeared against the wet paving. Tamsin moved it, then asked her to try again.\n\nNeri posted three notices: the approved operating conditions, the name of each duty worker, and a private route for residents to report flooding. Nobody would have to post a home address or a medical need beside the bakery menu. Two people outside the repair crew would review disputes.\n\n\"That seems rather formal,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Water can be extremely formal when it reaches somebody's kitchen,\" Sela replied.\n\nEven Danse smiled.\n\nA child asked whether the new channel would stay forever. Fen crouched beside the diagram.\n\n\"We don't know. We test things, fix them, and sometimes replace them.\"\n\n\"So something can work without being finished?\"\n\n\"Especially that,\" Toph said.\n\nWe put the tools away before rain arrived. The unknown signature in Moonroot's Closed Bells records was still unknown, and no newly approved surface test gave us permission to cross the old underground barrier. But for tonight Sela could go home with a dry garden and Edda could use the path. That felt worth celebrating properly.\n\n\nThe lower passage remained closed.\n\nThat evening, Maui found me sitting on the bench above Tideglass. I was tired enough that the sea seemed to move in slow pieces.\n\n\"Disappointed?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Because we didn't open the old route?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"But the gardens are safer,\" I said.\n\n\"And people can use the path.\"\n\n\"And the inspection records are public.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Not exactly a great legend.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should stop measuring our lives by whether they make good legends.\"\n\nHe pretended to consider.\n\n\"That sounds like more work.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe kissed me softly.\n\nBelow us, the water moved through the new surface channel, doing what water had always tried to do.\n\nFinding somewhere safe to go.","word_count":2097}}};for(const s of lib.series){const dict=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const update=dict[ch.id];if(update&&update.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.content=update.content;ch.title=update.title;ch.word_count=update.word_count;}}s.total_word_count=s.chapters.reduce((sum,ch)=>sum+(Number(ch.word_count)||0),0);}})();
